2.3 Multidimensional Signal Processing Option--Questec

The main activity during this period has been an ongoing investigation
into a dataflow model called Regular Dataflow graphs (RDFGs). RDFGs
compactly represent multidimensional algorithms with regular control
and data flow, and thus match multidimensional signal processing
applications well. We hope to use them to improve the ability of
multidimensional dataflow representations in Ptolemy to exploit
parallel signal processing hardware.

RDFGs have been studied before, although unsatisfactorily, in the
context of wavefront array processors. RDFGs are distinguished by
their highly regular structure; this regularity leads to descriptions
that are exponentially more compact than descriptions of normal graphs.
Hence, very large graphs can be described using very little space, and
infinite graphs can be described using finite space (infinite graphs
arise as precedence graphs in signal processing where the time
dimension is infinite). RDFGs can model algorithms where the structure
of the algorithm leads to highly homogeneous processing elements and
activities, like matrix multiplication. RDFGs can also be used to model
the precedence graphs of multidimensional data flow graphs (MD DFG),
which is our practical motivation for studying them. 

We have been interested in designing certain graph-theoretic algorithms
for RDFGs that arise commonly in design prototyping environments. The
goal is to have algorithms that work on the compact description, and
hence take much less time than conventional algorithms that work on
traditional graph descriptions (like incidence matrices and adjacency
lists). Our first results in this area are discouraging in the sense
that problems such as cycle detection and deadlock detection turn out
to be NP-complete (proven in a forthcoming technical report). However,
the NP-completeness arises when the size of the problem increases in
certain dimensions; practically, the increase in these dimensions does
not occur much. Hence, we have been pursuing heuristics, and the
possibility of polynomial time algorithms when the particular
dimensions that cause NP-completeness are assumed to be non-increasing
(usually true in practice). A concrete example of such a "dimension" is
the number of arcs that each node has in an RDFG. Usually, this is a
fixed small number such as 4 or 5, and does not increase even if the
number of nodes is very large. 

Another problem of interest is the computation of the maximum cycle
mean; this quantity determines the maximum rate at which the graph can
be executed and is thus metric used for designing and evaluating
multiprocessor schedulers.

Ultimately, having such algorithms will enable us to better analyze the
(infinite) precedence graphs that arise in MD DFGs. This in turn, will
allow us to develop good multiprocessor schedulers for multidimensional
dataflow in Ptolemy, something that is currently not there. 

Since RDFGs are described using integer matrices, and cycles can be
described using non-negative, integral vectors in the null space of
these matrices, the theory of lattices and integer linear algebra is
relevant here. We are engaged in deciphering the useful aspects of this
theory, to see how it can be applied in our context. An example of
integer linear algebra is the problem of computing linear dependence or
independence of a set of integral vectors by *non-negative integral*
coefficients (contrasted with linear algebra where the coefficients can
be real numbers). This naturally requires the use of results from the
study of Diophantine equations and geometric lattices. 


2.4 Software Improvements

Christopher Hylands improved the handling Java files within Tycho under
Windows NT so that Java files can now be compiled and executed entirely
from within Tycho. A major part of the challenge here was to develop a
rational and robust handling the CLASSPATH environment variable that
Java depends on.

Christopher Hylands created a facility in Tycho for generating class
inheritance diagrams from C++ source code. This complements the
facility we previously wrote in Tycho that generates class diagrams
from Itcl source code.

Mudit Goel completed a modification of Ptolemy that improves the
modeling of time in combined discrete-event and dataflow applications.
In particular, previously, a dataflow subsystem within a discrete-event
simulation had to be supplied with clocking events to trigger its
firings. With Mudit's change, the dataflow subsystem can directly
specify that it requires repeated periodic firing. This allows it to
more naturally model real-time signal sources using dataflow.

Christopher Hylands created a search facility in Tycho fashioned after
"egrep," a Unix program, and integrated a directory search mechanism
into Tycho. This provides a portable mechanism with a uniform interface
across platforms (Windows '95, Windows NT, and Unix).

Christopher Hylands added a mechanism to Tycho that will convert a Unix
style makefile to a Microsoft Visual C++ makefile. This will help in
the construction of a more complete port of Ptolemy to Windows.

Edward Lee and Seehyun Kim implemented a gridded, multi-column user
query widget in Tycho. The first application is a dialog whereby the
user guides the selection of fixed-point properties of a signal
processing system described with a dataflow graph.

Brian Evans and Guy Maor (UT Austin, under subcontract) installed a new
Matlab interface that removes dependencies on the Matlab 5 shared
library in the Ptolemy executables. It also works with Matlab 4.2 and
works when Matlab is not present.

John Reekie created a widget class called MultiPanel in Tycho that
provides hierarchically nested pull-down panels with arbitrary content.

Seehyun Kim wrote some C++ code to collect statistics of inputs,
outputs, and states of a functional block in the dataflow domains.
Statistics might be used for estimating signal ranges to help in
generating a fixed-point implementation of a system. He modified the
Tycho editor class EditPtlang to translate a block definition without
range estimation into one with range estimation. He has also modified
the Ptolemy preprocessor ptlang to support the C++ classes implementing
range estimation.


